1. WHAT IS AUTO VALIDATOR?
--------------------------

Auto Validator checks that a new user has a working email address, then
upgrades their account automatically.

  1. A new user enters Auto Validator and types their email address.
  2. Auto Validator emails them a four-character validation code.
  3. The user calls back, enters Auto Validator again, and types the code.
  4. Auto Validator validates them: it can switch them to a new class,
     give them a key, and post credits - whatever you set up for the
     validation method.

Auto Validator validates by email only.  The phone call-back and postal
mail methods of version 2.0 have been retired.


2. INSTALLING
-------------

  1. Copy GALAV.DLL, GALAV.MDF, GALAV.MSG and GALAV.RLN into your BBS
     directory.
  2. Run the Menu Tree editor and add Auto Validator to your menu.
  3. Make sure your Internet mail (SMTP) gateway is set up and can send
     mail out - Auto Validator sends codes through it.
  4. Start the BBS.

On first start Auto Validator creates its data files:

  GALAVUSR.DAT   one record per user who has used Auto Validator
  GALAVSET.DAT   Auto Validator's settings

UPGRADING FROM 2.1.0 OR 2.0: up to version 2.1.0 the module's files
were named HVSAV.*; from 2.2.0 they are named GALAV.*.

  1. With the BBS down, DELETE the old HVSAV.DLL, HVSAV.MDF, HVSAV.MSG
     and HVSAV.MCV.  Leaving them in place would load both versions.
  2. Copy in the new GALAV.* files as above.  Your menu entry for Auto
     Validator keeps working: menus find a module by its name, which
     has not changed.
  3. Keep your data files.  On first start Auto Validator renames
     HVSAVUSR.DAT to GALAVUSR.DAT, HVSAVSET.DAT to GALAVSET.DAT and
     HVSAVBAD.TXT to GALAVBAD.TXT (each noted in the Audit Trail), so
     validation records, pending codes and settings carry over.  An
     activity log you named in the settings keeps its name.

From 2.0 only: HVSAVCFG.DAT and HVSAVNVR.DAT (phone settings and the
"never call" list) are no longer used and can be deleted.  The
activation code is gone; no registration is needed.


3. SETTING IT UP
----------------

Settings are changed ONLINE, from the Auto Validator sysop menu, and take
effect immediately - no restart.  The options in the configuration editor
(GALAV.MSG, levels 3 and 4) are only the install defaults: they are copied
into GALAVSET.DAT the first time the module starts.  To copy them in
again later, use "Reset all settings" in the sysop menu.

Enter Auto Validator with the sysop key (SYSOP by default) to get the
sysop menu:

  S  Settings        - change any setting below
  E  Validation email - edit the email's subject and body (section 4)
  L  Look up a user  - see a user's validation record
  G  Global commands - list the sysop commands (section 6)
  X  Exit

Each settings change is written to the Audit Trail and the activity log.

GENERAL SETTINGS

  Key needed to request validation   Only users with this key may use
                                     Auto Validator.  Blank = anyone.
  Key for sysop menu and commands    Users with this key get the sysop
                                     menu and commands, and are never
                                     validated themselves.  Blank = SYSOP.
  Method used for email validations  Which method (1-5) a user gets when
                                     they enter a correct code.
  Max validated users per email      How many validated users may share
                                     one email address.
  Failed validations allowed         Every three wrong codes count as
                                     one failure, even across visits.
                                     After this many failures the user
                                     must be reset by a sysop.  0 = no
                                     limit (not recommended).
  Offer validation at logon          YES: unvalidated users are asked at
                                     logon whether to validate now.
  Global command trigger             First character of the sysop
                                     commands (default @).
  Email type shown to users          How email is described to users,
                                     e.g. "Internet".
  Internet email address prefix      Put in front of the user's address
                                     to route the message out through
                                     your Internet mail gateway
                                     (default IN:).  Must match the
                                     prefix your SMTP gateway uses.
  Validation email sent from         The From address of the validation
                                     email, e.g. validate@example.com or
                                     validate@bbs.example.com.  Blank =
                                     the Sysop, which the gateway sends
                                     as sysop@ its host name.
  Validation email subject           The subject line.  [NAME] stands
                                     for a text variable (section 4).
  Email domain refused               A domain users may not validate
                                     with - usually your own BBS's.
  Activity log file                  Text log of every validation,
                                     failure and sysop action.  Blank =
                                     no log.  Default GALAV.LOG.  Online,
                                     only a plain file name (in the BBS
                                     directory) is accepted.

VALIDATION METHODS 1-5

Each method has four settings:

  Class to switch to    The class the user is switched to.  It must exist
                        in your Accounts setup - Auto Validator will not
                        let you choose a class that does not exist, and
                        skips the switch (with an Audit Trail warning) if
                        the class is later removed.  Blank = no change.
  Key to give           A key added to the user.  Blank = none.
  Credits to post       Credits added to the account.  0 = none.
  Credit type           FREE or PAID, for your accounting.

REFUSING ADDRESSES

Besides the refused domain setting, you can create GALAVBAD.TXT in the
BBS directory with one entry per line.  Any address that contains one of
the entries (upper or lower case) is refused.  For example:

     MAILINATOR.COM
     @MYBBS.EXAMPLE


4. THE VALIDATION EMAIL
-----------------------

The email is sent through your Internet mail (SMTP) gateway.

FROM ADDRESS.  Set "Validation email sent from" (Settings, General) to
the address users should see, such as validate@example.com.  It must be
a full address.  Use whatever your domain's mail is set up for - your
host name or just your domain.  Choose an address that can receive
mail: bounces and replies go to it.  Leave it blank to send as the
Sysop, as versions before 2.2.0 did.

SUBJECT AND BODY.  Choose "Validation email" (E) in the sysop menu.  It
shows the current From address, subject and body, and offers:

  E  Edit the subject and body in the BBS editor.  The subject is the
     editor's topic line.  Save to use the new text from the next email
     sent; quit to leave it unchanged.
  R  Restore the default subject and body.

In the subject and the body, [NAME] is replaced by the BBS text variable
NAME when the email is sent:

  [GALAV_USERID]   the user's User-ID
  [GALAV_CODE]     the validation code - leave this in the body!
  [GALAV_EMAIL]    the user's email address
  [SYSTEM_NAME]    your system's name

Any text variable registered on your BBS may be used.  Text in brackets
that is not a text variable name is sent as typed.

The edited body is kept in GALAVEML.TXT in the BBS directory, a plain
text file you may also edit with any text editor.  Without that file
the default body is used: the YOUVAL message in GALAV.MSG (level 6).
The subject is a setting, so it can also be changed on the General
settings page.  The email is sent as plain text: color codes are
removed.


5. WHAT USERS SEE
-----------------

A new user entering Auto Validator is asked whether to begin email
validation, then for their email address.  The address must look like
name@example.com.  The code is emailed, and the user is told to come
back.

When they return, Auto Validator asks for the code.  Every three wrong
codes count as one failed validation, whether they come in one visit or
several.  A correct code validates them at once.

Users who are already validated, who lack the request key, or who have
used up their failed attempts are told so and returned to the menu.

The wording of every screen is in GALAV.MSG (level 6) and can be changed
in the configuration editor.  Values such as the user's address appear
as text variables (GALAV_EMAIL, GALAV_CODE, ...) that you may move or
remove.  The email itself is described in section 4.


6. SYSOP GLOBAL COMMANDS
------------------------

Usable from anywhere on the system by users with the sysop key.  Shown
here with the default trigger, @.

  @V n user   Validate a user with method n (1-5).
              Example: @V 1 Some User
  @D user     Devalidate: clears validated status, attempts and any
              pending code, so the user can validate again.  (Their
              class is not changed back.)
  @E user     Email the user their code again (a new code is made if
              they have none).
  @L user     Show the user's validation record.
  @O user     Toggle the override: an overridden user is not stopped by
              the failed-attempt or shared-address limits.
  @?          List these commands.


7. FILES
--------

  GALAV.DLL      the module
  GALAV.MDF      module description
  GALAV.MSG      text and install-default options (the BBS builds
                 GALAV.MCV from it)
  GALAV.RLN      this manual
  GALAVUSR.DAT   user validation records (created automatically)
  GALAVSET.DAT   settings (created automatically)
  GALAVEML.TXT   the edited email body (written by the sysop menu)
  GALAV.LOG      activity log (name set in the settings)
  GALAVBAD.TXT   optional list of refused addresses (you create it)
